  [PASS] 121d AGAINST INTEREST -- this does NOT decide between the two footings, and the reason is the distance scale.  The M/L-immunised a_0 lands on the canonical footing almost exactly, but its total error is dominated by a systematic the estimator cannot see: a coherent 10% error in SPARC's distances moves a_0 by 0.09 dex, which is larger than the whole 0.082 dex gap between the footings.  The central value favours canonical; the error bar does not exclude alt, and item 123 must be told so   (a_0(gas) = 9.333e-11 +- 0.116 dex total: canonical at 0.0 sigma, alt at 0.7 sigma.  Statistical alone would give canonical 0.0 sigma and alt 1.4 sigma -- so the distance scale, not the sample size, is what blocks the footing decision here)

  THE LADDER, RE-RUNG (input to items 123 and 125).  The item-100 ladder's two supposedly M/L-free rungs were item 25's deep tail (1.14e-10) and the KiDS dwarf lens stack (9.55e-11), 0.08 dex apart.  Item 25's rung is not M/L-free (leverage -0.65, above).  Replacing it with the immunised measurement made here:
     gas-dominated, M/L-immunised deep tail : 9.333e-11 +- 0.116 dex   (leverage -0.14)
     KiDS dwarf lens stack (item 2)         : 9.550e-11 +- 0.109 dex
     they agree to 0.010 dex, and both sit on the canonical footing (-0.001 and +0.009 dex).
     That agreement is far inside both error bars (0.12 and 0.11 dex), so it is a coincidence of central values and NOT a 0.01 dex measurement.  What it does support is that the ladder's spread was M/L, exactly as item 100 said.

======================================================================================================================
MUTATION CONTROLS
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M121-1 the transfer carries real information, shown by breaking it: calibrate a_0 on the TARGET galaxies instead -- the circular version the item is designed to avoid -- and both the a_0 and the recovered Upsilon move.  The gas-calibrated chain is therefore not returning its own input   (a_0 from the star-dominated targets themselves = 1.067e-10 (+0.058 dex from the gas-calibrated 9.333e-11), and it returns Upsilon = 0.508 against the transferred 0.534)
  a_0 x 0.5: predicted Upsilon median 0.693 (+0.142 dex from SPS), implied d log Upsilon/d log a_0 = -0.38
  a_0 x 2.0: predicted Upsilon median 0.386 (-0.112 dex from SPS), implied d log Upsilon/d log a_0 = -0.47
  [PASS] M121-2 the a_0 mutation, and it is honest about the POWER rather than flattering it.  Doubling a_0 moves the predicted Upsilon off the stellar-population value -- but by only 1.3 sigma of the stellar populations' own error, because these star-dominated galaxies sit at accelerations where the kernel's response to a_0 is half what the deep-MOND limit gives.  So 121b is a genuine zero-parameter agreement and NOT a sharp measurement of a_0: this test on its own constrains a_0 only to about a factor of two   (a_0 x 2 -> Upsilon = 0.386, -0.112 dex from SPS = 1.3 sigma of the SPS uncertainty; measured d log Upsilon/d log a_0 = -0.47, against -1 in the deep-MOND limit)
  [PASS] M121-3 the kernel is load-bearing: with nu = 1 and no dark matter the same target curves demand nearly twice the stellar-population mass-to-light ratio, so the agreement in 121b is not something the rotation curves would give for any gravity law   (Newtonian predicted Upsilon median 0.950 = +0.28 dex from SPS, against 0.534 = +0.029 dex with the kernel)
  [PASS] M121-4 pairing each target galaxy's photometry with a DIFFERENT galaxy's observed rotation curve, interpolated onto its own radial range, roughly triples the spread of the recovered Upsilon and rails a tenth of the fits.  So the 0.14 dex distribution in 121c is genuine per-galaxy correspondence between light and kinematics, not something the estimator would return for any pairing   (scrambled pairs over 20 permutations: N = 955 fits (136 railed and dropped, against 1 in the true sample), median Upsilon 0.568, spread 0.468 dex against the true 0.136 dex (ratio 3.4x) and the measurement budget 0.134)

