   canonical a_0 = 9.360e-11 imposed: predicted Upsilon median 0.534 (+0.029 dex from SPS) on the same 55 star-dominated galaxies
         alt a_0 = 1.130e-10 imposed: predicted Upsilon median 0.495 (-0.004 dex from SPS) on the same 55 star-dominated galaxies
  measured a_0(gas) = 9.333e-11 gives 0.534.  Item 76's deep-tail inversion required 0.656 / 0.504; item 119's full-curve median over a wider sample was 0.505 / 0.456.  The spread among these is the estimator-and-sample systematic on 'the Upsilon that Lambda predicts', and it is about 0.1 dex.
  AGAINST INTEREST, and it is why 121d refuses to pick a footing: read as an imposed-a_0 test the ALT footing lands closer to stellar populations here, while the MEASURED gas calibration lands on canonical.  Both differences are well inside the 0.12 dex systematic on a_0(gas), so the two readings do not conflict -- but neither may be quoted as favouring a footing, in either direction.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE LambdaCDM / NEWTONIAN ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  There is no LambdaCDM version of this chain.  A dark halo has at least two free parameters per galaxy, so the
  gas-dominated galaxies determine no acceleration scale to transfer, and the star-dominated ones' Upsilon is
  degenerate with the halo -- rotation curves bound it from above and no further.  What LambdaCDM does instead is
  take Upsilon from stellar populations as an INPUT and fit the halo; the framework takes Lambda as the input and
  returns Upsilon as an OUTPUT.  The comparison is therefore asymmetric by construction: this item shows that one
  theory makes the prediction and that the prediction is right, not that the other theory is wrong.
  the maximum-disc upper bound on Upsilon for these same 55 galaxies: median 0.48, 5-95% 0.08 - 1.09 -- an upper limit, and 45% of it lies above the stellar-population value, i.e. carries no information

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  The chain closes.  a_0 measured on 27 gas-dominated SPARC galaxies -- with a point-level cut that keeps only
  places where the stars supply under 30% of g_bar, and iterated to self-consistency -- is 9.333e-11 m/s^2,
  -0.001 dex from the canonical footing.  Transferred with no further freedom to 55 disjoint star-dominated
  galaxies, it predicts Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.534, +0.029 dex from stellar populations, with a spread of 0.14 dex
  that the distance and inclination errors alone account for.  Median and spread both pass.
  Three things are recorded AGAINST it.  (1) The power is modest: doubling a_0 would show at only 1.3 sigma of the
  stellar populations' own error, so this is a zero-parameter agreement, not a sharp measurement of a_0.  (2) The
  footing question is NOT settled -- a coherent 10% distance-scale error moves a_0 by 0.09 dex, more than the gap
  between the footings.  (3) The scatter agreement is a bound, not a detection, for the same reason as in item 119.
  And one correction it forces on this hunt's own ledger: item 25's 1.14e-10 is NOT the M/L-free rung the item-100
  ladder calls it -- its Upsilon leverage is -0.65, the largest there -- and the immunised replacement is
  9.333e-11, which agrees with the dwarf lens stack to 0.010 dex instead of 0.08.

